// Type checking for the JavaScript the app still ships: the two preload bridges and the removal window.
//
// Nothing is compiled and nothing is emitted: this reads preload.js, preload-uninstall.js and
// renderer/uninstall.js with the JSDoc they carry and says where the code and its own
// documentation disagree. It used to read main.js and every module in src/ the same way, which is
// how it found a require that had never resolved and two handlers that answered every call with
// "Cannot find module". Since 2026-09-30 the main process is TypeScript and src/tsconfig.json
// checks it, strict.
//
// The preloads stay JavaScript on purpose: Electron runs them in the sandbox, as CommonJS, before
// the page, and a preload that failed to load would leave the window with no window.api at all.
// The removal window is a classic script loaded without a build (vite.config.mjs says why), and
// renderer/uninstall-bridge.d.ts describes what its preload gives it.
//
// Why these settings:
//   checkJs with strict off - the point is to catch what is wrong, not to fail on every value that
//     could be null in files that only pass messages through.
//   module preserve - each file is read the way it is written.
//   skipLibCheck - Electron's own .d.ts is not this project's to fix.
//
// The rest of renderer/ is TypeScript with a tsconfig of its own. Not covered: tools/, a mix of both
// module systems that needs its own settings.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["preload.js", "preload-uninstall.js", "renderer/uninstall.js", "renderer/uninstall-bridge.d.ts"]
}
